## 3. Host-based routing without buying a domain

A public registered domain is not required for local host-based routing.

Host-based routing works because the browser sends an HTTP `Host` header. DNS/name resolution gets the client to the server IP; the Ingress Controller then uses the hostname in the HTTP request to choose a backend.

Example request:

```http
GET / HTTP/1.1
Host: grafana.k3s.test
```

For the local lab, add these lines to the **Windows hosts file**, opened with Administrator privileges:

File:

```text
C:\Windows\System32\drivers\etc\hosts
```

Entries:

```text
10.227.142.162 app.k3s.test
10.227.142.162 argocd.k3s.test
10.227.142.162 grafana.k3s.test
10.227.142.162 prometheus.k3s.test
```

Then run in an elevated PowerShell window:

```powershell
ipconfig /flushdns
```

The `.test` hostnames are local test names, not registered public DNS records. The hosts file only provides name-to-IP resolution; it does not open ports, configure routing, or secure the applications.

### URLs in the current lab

Because the NGINX controller Pod is configured with `hostPort: 80` and `hostPort: 443`, the browser can use the standard HTTP/HTTPS ports without showing a port number:

```text
http://app.k3s.test/
http://argocd.k3s.test/
http://grafana.k3s.test/
http://prometheus.k3s.test/
```

The controller Service also exposes NodePorts. Those are a separate way to reach the same controller:

```text
http://app.k3s.test:30143/
https://app.k3s.test:32132/
```

Use the appropriate protocol and certificate configuration before using HTTPS. The HTTPS NodePort existing does not, by itself, mean a valid TLS certificate has been configured.

