## 9. Argo CD configuration for this lab

Argo CD normally serves HTTPS on its server endpoint. In this lab, NGINX is forwarding HTTP to the `argocd-server` Service on port `80`, so the Argo CD server was configured to run without its own TLS layer:

```yaml
data:
  server.insecure: "true"
```

Edit the ConfigMap:

```bash
kubectl edit configmap argocd-cmd-params-cm -n argocd
```

For host-based routing at `/`, do not retain old subpath settings such as:

```yaml
server.rootpath: /argocd
server.basehref: /argocd
```

Remove those explicit old subpath entries if they are present. The default root path is appropriate for `http://argocd.k3s.test/`.

Restart and check the server Deployment after changing the ConfigMap:

```bash
kubectl rollout restart deployment/argocd-server -n argocd
kubectl rollout status deployment/argocd-server -n argocd
```

**Command correction:** `kubectl edit` opens the resource for editing and does not need `-o yaml`. To view YAML without editing, use:

```bash
kubectl get configmap argocd-cmd-params-cm -n argocd -o yaml
```

### Argo CD initial admin password

```bash
kubectl -n argocd get secret argocd-initial-admin-secret \
  -o jsonpath='{.data.password}' | base64 -d; echo
```

This is the initial admin password Secret. It may no longer exist if it was deleted after the initial password was changed. Do not expose the decoded password.

### Lab vs production

`server.insecure: "true"` disables TLS on the Argo CD server endpoint. It is a common arrangement when a trusted ingress proxy terminates TLS, but this lab is currently HTTP-only. For a production deployment, use HTTPS from the browser to the ingress and make an explicit decision about encryption between the ingress and Argo CD. If backend TLS is required, configure the ingress/backend protocol accordingly rather than blindly enabling insecure mode.

