## 10. Update Grafana and Prometheus settings through Helm

The Grafana and Prometheus URL settings are both managed through the existing `kube-prometheus-stack` Helm release. Your release name is:

```text
prometheus-stack
```

### Step 1 — Back up the current user-supplied values

```bash
helm get values prometheus-stack -n monitoring -o yaml \
  > prometheus-stack-user-values-backup.yaml
```

Check the installed chart version:

```bash
helm list -n monitoring
```

The `CHART` column may show something like:

```text
kube-prometheus-stack-92.14.0
```

For `helm upgrade --version`, use only the version number (`92.14.0`), not the full chart identifier.

### Step 2 — Create a small override file

Create `prometheus-local-values.yaml`:

```yaml
grafana:
  env:
    GF_SERVER_ROOT_URL: "http://grafana.k3s.test/"
    GF_SERVER_SERVE_FROM_SUB_PATH: "false"

prometheus:
  prometheusSpec:
    externalUrl: "http://prometheus.k3s.test"
    routePrefix: "/"
```

If you are using NodePort URLs instead of HostPort, include `:30143` in both HTTP URLs. If you later use HTTPS on the standard port 443, use `https://` and omit the port.

### Step 3 — Update the existing Helm release

First ensure the chart repository exists and update its index if needed:

```bash
helm repo list
helm repo update
```

Then upgrade the **existing** release, preserving previous user-supplied values and applying the new overrides:

```bash
helm upgrade prometheus-stack prometheus-community/kube-prometheus-stack \
  --namespace monitoring \
  --version 92.14.0 \
  --reuse-values \
  -f prometheus-local-values.yaml \
  --wait \
  --timeout 10m
```

Use `--version 92.14.0` only if `helm list -n monitoring` confirms that is the installed chart version. Otherwise substitute the version shown by Helm. `--reuse-values` retains the previous release's user values and merges the new file; the new values take precedence for keys they define.

**Do not use this:**

```bash
--version kube-prometheus-stack-92.14.0
```

That includes the chart name and is not a valid version constraint. Use only:

```bash
--version 92.14.0
```

### Step 4 — Verify the values and reconciled Prometheus resource

```bash
helm get values prometheus-stack -n monitoring -o yaml

kubectl get prometheus prometheus-stack-kube-prom-prometheus \
  -n monitoring \
  -o jsonpath='ExternalURL: {.spec.externalUrl}{"\n"}RoutePrefix: {.spec.routePrefix}{"\n"}'

kubectl get pods -n monitoring -o wide
```

The Helm chart updates the Prometheus custom resource; the Prometheus Operator then reconciles the running Prometheus workload. Do not edit the generated StatefulSet directly as the persistent configuration method.

