## 11. Useful troubleshooting commands

### Check the Ingress resources

```bash
kubectl get ingress -A -o wide
kubectl describe ingress prometheus-ingress -n monitoring
```

### Check Services and backend endpoints

```bash
kubectl get svc -n monitoring
kubectl get endpoints prometheus-stack-kube-prom-prometheus -n monitoring
kubectl get svc -n app-ns
```

A Service existing does not guarantee it has ready endpoints. Check endpoints when NGINX returns an upstream error.

### Check NGINX controller logs

```bash
kubectl logs -n ingress-nginx \
  deployment/ingress-nginx-controller --tail=100
```

### Test host routing without relying on Windows DNS

Use the `Host` header explicitly:

```bash
curl -i -H 'Host: app.k3s.test' \
  http://10.227.142.162:30143/

curl -i -H 'Host: grafana.k3s.test' \
  http://10.227.142.162:30143/
```

To test Prometheus Basic Auth:

```bash
# Without credentials: a 401 challenge is expected
curl -i -H 'Host: prometheus.k3s.test' \
  http://10.227.142.162:30143/-/healthy

# With credentials: curl prompts for the password
curl -i -u admin -H 'Host: prometheus.k3s.test' \
  http://10.227.142.162:30143/-/healthy
```

If testing through HostPort instead of NodePort, use `http://10.227.142.162/` and omit `:30143`.

### Interpreting common HTTP responses

| Response | Typical meaning / next check |
|---|---|
| `200 OK` | Request reached an application endpoint successfully. |
| `301` / `302` | The application or proxy redirected the request. Inspect the `Location` header; confirm the target URL/path is correct. |
| `401 Unauthorized` | Authentication is required or credentials were rejected. For Prometheus, a 401 without credentials is expected. |
| `403 Forbidden` | The request was understood but access was denied; inspect allowlists/auth/authorization. |
| `404 Not Found` | Wrong host/path, or the backend application does not serve that path. Check the application's own URL/path settings as well as the Ingress rule. |
| `503 Service Unavailable` | Often an upstream Service/endpoint/port problem, or an invalid NGINX auth Secret/configuration. Inspect controller logs and endpoint data; do not assume one cause. |

### Prometheus-specific checks

Your observed Prometheus Service and endpoint were:

```text
Service:  prometheus-stack-kube-prom-prometheus
Port:     9090
Endpoint: 10.42.0.154:9090 (Pod IP can change)
```

The Prometheus custom resource previously showed stale path-based settings:

