## 13. Public EC2 without an AWS ALB or NLB

A self-managed single-node k3s/EC2 setup can expose NGINX directly through the EC2 instance's public IP. One possible traffic path is:

```text
Internet client
    |
    v
DNS A record / DDNS name -> EC2 public IP
    |
    v
EC2 Security Group (TCP 80/443)
    |
    v
NGINX Controller exposed on node ports 80/443
    |
    v
Kubernetes Services (normally ClusterIP)
    |
    v
Application Pods
```

No AWS ALB or NLB is required for that single-node traffic path. HostPort is one way to expose the controller on node ports 80/443; NodePort or k3s ServiceLB are other mechanisms with different traffic paths.

For an EC2 deployment:

- Use a stable public IP (for example, an Elastic IP) if the DNS record must remain stable.
- Allow inbound TCP 80/443 only for the intended public web entry point.
- Restrict SSH access to approved administrator IPs or use SSM where available.
- Do not expose the full Kubernetes NodePort range to the internet.
- Keep Argo CD and monitoring private or protect them with strong network restrictions and identity controls.
- A single EC2 instance and single ingress controller are a single point of failure; this is not highly available merely because Kubernetes is running on it.

A DDNS record only maps a hostname to an IP address. It is not a load balancer, firewall, TLS certificate, authentication system, or access-control mechanism.

